# Four properties of every code point in Unicode 17.0.0 that IDNA's checks
# read and `unicode` does not offer: the bidirectional class (RFC 5893), the
# joining type and the canonical combining class (RFC 5892, Appendix A), and
# whether a character may stand in text normalized to NFC (UAX #15's quick
# check). `lookup.hero` reads it.
#
# The table is data, made and not typed: a one-off program read the Unicode
# Character Database 17.0.0 of https://www.unicode.org/Public/17.0.0/ucd/ on
# 2026-11-17, `extracted/DerivedBidiClass.txt` (SHA-256
# 4867b4b7f0731ed1...), `extracted/DerivedJoiningType.txt`
# (f39ebe974825d673...), `extracted/DerivedCombiningClass.txt`
# (191463abfbd20270...) and `DerivedNormalizationProps.txt`
# (71fd6a206a2c0cdd...), their `@missing` defaults
# included, held the bidirectional and combining classes to Python 3.15.2's
# `unicodedata` (17.0.0) for every assigned code point, reduced the four to
# runs, held every lookup to the files for each of the 1,114,112 code points,
# and wrote this module whole. Nothing here is edited by hand.

# The runs of one set of the four properties, each `first << 21 | bidi << 14 |
# joining << 11 | quick << 8 | combining`. The code points from `first` up to
# the next run's share them. `bidi` is 0 L, 1 R, 2 AL, 3 AN, 4 EN, 5 ES, 6 CS,
# 7 ET, 8 ON, 9 BN, 10 NSM, and 11 for every class RFC 5893 does not name;
# `joining` is 0 for none (U, and C, which RFC 5892 does not read), 1 L, 2 D,
# 3 R, 4 T; `quick` is NFC_Quick_Check, 0 yes, 1 maybe, 2 no; `combining` is
# the canonical combining class, 0 to 254, 9 being a virama. The first run
# begins at 0 and the runs ascend.
constant RUNS: [i64]
